3. Modifications to src/solver/ (DistQLDPC patches)
-----------------------------------------------------

Copyright (C) 2025-2026 Yu-Fang Chen <yfc@iis.sinica.edu.tw>
Part of DistQLDPC (GPL-3.0-or-later when distributed as this project).

These changes are documented in detail in MODIFICATIONS.md. Summary:

  - Bounds pipe API (TRY / LB / UB messages for parent-process progress)
  - Best-solution tracking and printing on timeout / interrupt
  - Configurable cardinality encoding modes (Sinz / MTO / off)
  - Retire inactive unit and auxiliary soft literals after preprocessing
    partition, preserving representative clauses and objective accounting (GH58)
  - Multi-instance search controls and per-instance heuristic state (interleaved CSS split, experimental)
  - GH-148 (inactive unless DistQLDPC passes rows; it does by default on the split MaxSAT path): native "at most k of these
    variables" propagator for stabilizer-overlap rows in propagate() / propagateForLK(), with
    lazily allocated, cached reason clauses (relocated in relocAll, skipped by on-the-fly reduction)

Modified files (partial list): Solver.h, Solver.cc

  src/core/distqldpc.cc also contains GH-85 per-half symmetry breaking
  (GH-73 x GH-75 interaction): an original, domain-specific adaptation of
  static symmetry breaking (Crawford et al., KR 1996; Anders, Brenner, Rattan,
  "Satsuma", SAT 2024, DOI 10.4230/LIPIcs.SAT.2024.4). No third-party code.
  src/core/distqldpc.cc also contains GH-87 dual-half elimination (an
  original use of verified XZ-dual qubit permutations to prove dX = dZ in
  the GH-73 split; GF(2) checks reused from GH-75). No third-party code.
  src/core/distqldpc.cc also contains GH-107 dual-map bound sharing (an
  original use of the verified XZ-dual maps of GH-87 to share proven lower
  bounds between the two GH-73 split halves; ported from GH-102). No
  third-party code.

  src/cc/ (GH-124, optional connected-cluster engine, off by default;
  enable with -engine=cc) implements the connected-cluster distance
  search of I. Dumer, A. A. Kovalev, L. P. Pryadko, "Distance
  verification for classical and quantum LDPC codes", IEEE Trans. Inf.
  Theory 63(7), 4675-4686, 2017. It was written from scratch from the
  published algorithm and DistQLDPC's own code. The search skeleton
  (connected clusters grown from a seed, branching on one unsatisfied
  check, closed-cluster dead ends, interleaved X/Z weight levels) was
  studied in Quantinuum's Qubitserf (https://github.com/Quantinuum/Qubitserf,
  Copyright (c) 2026 Serban Cercelescu and the qubitserf contributors,
  MIT License); no Qubitserf code was copied. DistQLDPC's own additions
  are the disjoint odd-check packing bound, sibling exclusion, orbit
  seeds from the GH-85 verified automorphisms, single-half search under
  the GH-87/GH-107 verified XZ-dual maps, the stop rule, independent
  witness verification and the verified basis-row upper bound (see
  README, "Connected-cluster (CC) engine").

  src/cc/cc_aut.h, src/cc/cc_aut.cc (GH-152, -cc-aut=on, the CC default
  since 2026-10-28) find candidate automorphisms of the coloured Tanner
  graph by colour refinement (coarsest equitable partition) and
  individualisation-refinement with backtracking, in the style of the
  graph-automorphism tools nauty and Traces (B. D. McKay, "Practical
  graph isomorphism", Congressus Numerantium 30, 45-87, 1981; B. D.
  McKay and A. Piperno, "Practical graph isomorphism, II", J. Symbolic
  Computation 60, 94-112, 2014) and bliss (T. Junttila, P. Kaski,
  ALENEX 2007). It was implemented from scratch from the published
  descriptions; no nauty, Traces, bliss or other third-party code was
  read, copied or linked. Every candidate is accepted only after
  DistQLDPC's own GF(2) checks (GH-75/GH-85/GH-87).

