7. Repetition-product hypergraph-product matrices (GH-153, untiered)
-----------------------------------------------------------------

Location: data/matrices/untiered/HGP_*; generator scripts/codegen/hgp_repetition.py.
Original clean-room implementation and generated matrices, GPL-3.0-or-later.
No third-party code, matrix files, or paper text were copied.

Construction credit: Jean-Pierre Tillich and Gilles Zemor, "Quantum LDPC
Codes With Positive Rate and Minimum Distance Proportional to the Square
Root of the Blocklength", arXiv:0903.0566v2 (2013), Theorem 1;
IEEE Transactions on Information Theory (2014), DOI 10.1109/TIT.2013.2292061.
The repetition-code specializations have claimed parameters
[[13,1,3]], [[41,1,5]], [[113,1,8]], [[265,1,12]], [[481,1,16]], [[1105,1,24]].
These are mathematical ground-truth claims, not DistQLDPC solver results.
All stems remain _unknown pending PI naming/tier approval. Short smoke runs
are recorded in GH-153: four exact reproductions, one bounded timeout, and
one default timeout without bounds (supplemental -ub-pre=1 gives 9..26).
See data/matrices/untiered/MANIFEST.csv and README.md for full provenance.

8. Nonzero-rate cycle-factor HGP matrices (GH-154, untiered)
----------------------------------------------------------

Original clean-room generator scripts/codegen/hgp_cycle.py and generated
HGP_{45,106,306,610,1261,2866}_* matrices, GPL-3.0-or-later. No graph data,
third-party code or source text copied. Uses the Tillich-Zemor HGP theorem
cited in section 7, with full-row-rank checks of graph cycle codes.
Graph mathematics is credited to the standard complete graph, Petersen,
